// batch aa: Paraguayan War (riachuelo, tuiuti, curupaiti, humaita, avai) and Magdala
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "canhoneira": "A small, shallow-draft ship with a few guns, built to operate on rivers and near the coast.",
  "chata": "A flat-bottomed barge with no engine, towed by steamers. In the Paraguayan War, some carried a single heavy gun and served as floating batteries.",
  "abalroar": "To strike another ship's hull with the bow of your own in order to damage or sink it. The same as using the ship as a ram.",
  "paiol": "The compartment where gunpowder is stored. If a ship's magazine explodes, the whole ship goes up.",
  "artilharia revólver": "A nickname given to Lieutenant Colonel Mallet's guns at Tuiuti, because they fired so fast and in so many directions that they looked like a giant revolver.",
  "foguete de congreve": "A war rocket invented by the Englishman William Congreve in the early 19th century. It had a range of about 2 km, flew erratically, and was worth more for the scare than for its aim.",
  "estero": "A Spanish word for low, marshy ground with standing water and grass, common in Paraguay and northern Argentina. In southern Brazil, a banhado.",
  "chaco": "A vast, hot, marshy plain on the right bank of the Paraguay River, opposite Humaitá and Asunción. It had almost no roads and was reputed to be impassable.",
  "cólera": "A disease caught by drinking contaminated water, which kills by dehydration. In the crowded camps of the Paraguayan War, it killed more people than many battles did.",
  "refém": "A person held prisoner to force the other side to give in or to negotiate.",
  "morteiro": "A short, wide-barreled cannon that fires high, so that the shot drops from above and clears walls.",
  "tabot": "A sacred replica of the Ark of the Covenant, kept in every Ethiopian Orthodox church. Only priests may see it, which is why the tabots taken by the British are one of the most sensitive points in the request for their return."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  riachuelo: {
    war: "Paraguayan War (1864–1870)",
    hook: "On a Sunday morning, the Brazilian Navy turned a badly begun battle into a victory, using its own ship as a weapon.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Paraguay of Francisco Solano López has no outlet to the sea. Everything it sells and buys passes along the Paraguay and Paraná rivers, and it was on those rivers that the war began. In November 1864, López ordered the capture of a Brazilian steamer, the Marquês de Olinda, and in December he invaded Mato Grosso. In April 1865, after Argentina refused permission to cross the province of Corrientes, he occupied the city of Corrientes itself.",
        "The answer was the [[Triple Alliance|tríplice aliança]]: on 1 May 1865, Brazil, Argentina and Uruguay signed a treaty against López. Brazil had a weapon Paraguay could not match, its fleet, and the plan was to [[blockade|bloqueio naval]] the rivers and strangle the country.",
        { h: "The road to Riachuelo" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Nov. 1864", "López captures the Brazilian steamer Marquês de Olinda. It is the start of the war."],
          ["Dec. 1864", "The Paraguayans invade Mato Grosso."],
          ["Apr. 1865", "Paraguayan troops occupy Corrientes, in Argentina."],
          ["1 May 1865", "The Triple Alliance treaty is signed."],
          ["10 June 1865", "At night, the Paraguayan fleet leaves Humaitá and heads downriver toward Corrientes."],
          ["11 June 1865", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "The Brazilian fleet was anchored on the Paraná, near Corrientes. For López it was the greatest threat: if the ships controlled the river, the Paraguayan army in the region would be cut off."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Francisco Manoel Barroso, Brazilian commander" },
        "A division chief of the Imperial Navy, he commanded the fleet aboard the frigate Amazonas. Admiral Tamandaré, the overall chief of the naval force, had sent him upriver. At Riachuelo, when the Brazilian line broke up, Barroso turned the Amazonas itself into a weapon: instead of just firing, he began ramming enemy ships.",
        { h: "Pedro Ignacio Meza, Paraguayan commander" },
        "A navy captain, he commanded the Paraguayan fleet aboard the Tacuarí. López's order was to arrive before dawn, place each steamer alongside a Brazilian ship and take it by boarding, with infantry soldiers aboard. Meza departed from that order on the spot and chose to steam past, firing. He was wounded in the chest and died eight days later, in the hospital at Humaitá.",
        { h: "López" },
        "He wanted to capture the Brazilian ships and bring them back. The idea of a surprise boarding depended on arriving at dawn with the fleet together, and that did not happen: one of the steamers broke down on the way and delayed them all."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Brazil had nine steamships and some 58 or 59 guns, depending on the source: the frigate Amazonas, four corvettes (Belmonte, Jequitinhonha, Beberibe and Parnaíba) and four [[gunboats|canhoneira]] (Ipiranga, Araguari, Iguatemi and Mearim). They carried about 2,300 men, counting sailors, marines and army soldiers.",
        "On the Paraguayan side, eight steamers armed with 38 or 39 guns, plus seven [[chatas|chata]] (sources say six or seven), each with one heavy gun. On land, on the bluff of the Riachuelo, Lieutenant Colonel Bruguez had about 22 guns, two batteries of [[Congreve rockets|foguete de congreve]] and about 1,200 men. A ninth steamer, the Yberá, broke its engine on the way and fell behind, which delayed the fleet.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Paraná is wide there, but the navigable channel is narrow near the mouth of the Riachuelo stream, between sandbanks and an island. Anyone passing through it came within a few dozen meters of the bluffs, from which the Paraguayan guns fired downward.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the delay spoiled the plan. The Paraguayan fleet was supposed to arrive before dawn, with the Brazilians asleep. It arrived only after 9 in the morning, on a Trinity Sunday when the ships were getting ready for Mass. Instead of boarding, Meza chose to steam past, firing.",
        "Second, at 9:25 the Paraguayans opened fire. The Brazilians weighed anchor and replied. Some chatas were hit, and a Paraguayan steamer, the Jejuy, was badly damaged. Then the Paraguayans went on downriver, passed the Brazilians, turned, and anchored the chatas crosswise in the channel, near the shore batteries, to close the passage.",
        "Third, the Brazilian line broke up. Sources differ on the details of these maneuvers. Barroso went down the river to attack, and the Belmonte, in front, took fire from everything there was and ran aground on an island. The Jequitinhonha also ran aground. The Parnaíba, which left the formation to help her, hit a bank and lost her rudder.",
        "Fourth, the most dangerous moment. Three Paraguayan steamers boarded the Parnaíba, and the Brazilian flag was even lowered. There was about an hour of hand-to-hand fighting on the deck, in which Midshipman Greenhalgh, the sailor Marcílio Dias and others died. According to the accounts, the commander ordered the [[magazine|paiol]] prepared for detonation rather than surrender the ship. The gunboats Araguari and Beberibe came to the rescue, and the Paraguayans fell back.",
        "Fifth, the turning point. Barroso turned the Amazonas around and [[rammed|abalroar]] one enemy ship after another, each with her iron bow. Sources differ on the order and on which ones sank: among those hit were the Paraguarí, the Salto Oriental, the Jejuy and the Marquês de Olinda, the very steamer López had captured in 1864. A chata also went down. The Parnaíba raised her flag again.",
        "Sixth, the end. The Paraguayan steamers still afloat fled upriver, pursued by the Beberibe and the Araguari until the end of the afternoon. The battle was over at about 5:30 p.m."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Paraguay lost four steamers and between four and seven chatas. Paraguayan casualties are between 750 and more than 900, depending on the source (one gives 351 dead and 567 wounded). Brazilian casualties were 104 dead, 123 to 142 wounded and 20 missing. Brazil lost one ship, the Jequitinhonha, which was burned to keep her from falling into Paraguayan hands.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Paraguayan fleet was never again an offensive force on the Paraná. Paraguay still controlled its own river, but it lost the chance to expel the fleet, and the allies came to control the River Plate basin up to the Paraguayan border, with supplies moving by water. It was the beginning of the turn in the war, which still had more than four years to run.",
        "Barroso became Baron of Amazonas, and 11 June is the Brazilian Navy's Data Magna.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The signal: the phrase 'Brazil expects every man to do his duty', which recalls Nelson's at Trafalgar, is attributed to Barroso. Some say the signal actually hoisted was a different one: 'Keep up the fire, for victory is ours'. Both versions circulate in the Brazilian sources." },
        { note: "The numbers: sources differ on how many ships and chatas Paraguay brought and on how many chatas sank. One counts eight steamers, another nine. The figures for people also vary widely from one source to another." },
        { note: "The death of Meza: according to one source, when López learned that Meza had died, he said that if he had not died of one shot, he should have died of four, and he forbade the officers to attend the burial." },
        { note: "The Marquês de Olinda and the Paraguarí: the first had been captured from the Brazilians in 1864 and fought for Paraguay. Of the Pirabebé, another Paraguayan steamer, restored parts survive, and the boiler and paddle wheel of the Yporá are also on display today." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  tuiuti: {
    war: "Paraguayan War (1864–1870)",
    hook: "The largest pitched battle in South America was decided by cannons hidden in a ditch.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Riachuelo, Paraguay lost the initiative on the river and ended up withdrawing from Corrientes. In September 1865, the Paraguayan army that had invaded Rio Grande do Sul surrendered at Uruguaiana. The allies were ready to enter Paraguay, and López knew that anyone attacking him had to cross swamps, forests and streams, terrain he knew and they did not.",
        { h: "The road to Tuiuti" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Sept. 1865", "The Paraguayans who invaded Rio Grande do Sul surrender at Uruguaiana."],
          ["Apr. 1866", "The allied army crosses the Paraná River and enters Paraguayan territory."],
          ["Early May 1866", "The Paraguayans attack at Estero Bellaco and are repulsed."],
          ["20 May 1866", "The allies advance and camp at Tuiuti."],
          ["24 May 1866", "López attacks the allied camp."]
        ]},
        "The allied camp occupied a strip of dry ground of about 4 by 2.4 km, cramped for 32,000 men. It was surrounded by [[esteros|estero]] and marshes. The allies had no good maps and did not know that the Paraguayans had a line of trenches, at Sauce, very close by.",
        "López learned that the allies intended to attack on the 25th. He decided to attack first, on the 24th."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Solano López" },
        "He planned a trap: to attack the camp at the same time from the sides and from behind, pushing the allies back toward the Paraná River. The plan was bold for the time, because the cavalry came first. But it had flaws: with no single command, the columns acted on their own, and one of them delayed the attack by about five hours.",
        { h: "Osório and Mitre" },
        "The Argentine general Bartolomé Mitre was the allied commander in chief. When the clash came, the man who decided the Brazilian side was General Manuel Luís Osório, who commanded the Brazilians: he rallied the fleeing soldiers, sent reserves to where the line was giving way, and sacrificed some battalions to keep the enemy from turning the camp. The Uruguayan general Venancio Flores commanded the Uruguayans, in the center.",
        { h: "Mallet and Sampaio" },
        "Lieutenant Colonel Emílio Luís Mallet, born in France, commanded the Brazilian artillery. The night before, he had a deep, hidden ditch dug in front of his guns. General Antônio de Sampaio, at the head of an infantry division, held the flank under fire and was wounded by a grenade fragment. He died of gangrene on 6 July, aboard a hospital ship.",
        { h: "The Paraguayan commanders" },
        "Four columns: José Eduvigis Díaz, in the center; Vicente Barrios, against the allied left, the Brazilians'; Francisco Isidoro Resquín, with the cavalry, against the right, the Argentines'; and Hilario Marcó, in reserve."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The allies had 32,000 to 35,000 men, depending on the source: about 21,000 to 22,000 Brazilians, 9,700 to 11,800 Argentines and some 1,200 to 1,300 Uruguayans. The allied cavalry did not exceed 1,700 men. The Paraguayans had between 24,000 and 26,000, with a lot of cavalry, about 8,500, but the sums of the four columns do not match the total, and a reserve of about 6,000 men did not reach the fighting.",
        { note: "No number for this battle is certain. Historians rely on accounts that disagree, and the lists of Paraguayan columns add up to more than the total given for the army." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A sandy, marshy plain of grass and low brush, hard to cross in order. The brush and the fog hid the Paraguayan approach.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the fog. The Paraguayans approached hidden by the brush and the mist. Around noon, a [[Congreve rocket|foguete de congreve]] went up and gave the signal for the attack.",
        "Second, the center gave way. Díaz's column fell on Flores's Uruguayans and on Brazilian volunteer battalions, and some soldiers fled.",
        "Third, Mallet's ditch. The Paraguayan cavalry, coming in a mass, stopped before the ditch, within range of the guns, which fired [[grapeshot|metralha]] in rapid, crossing fire. It was that rate of fire that gave the artillery the nickname [[revolver artillery|artilharia revólver]]. One source counts 28 guns, others speak of about 30.",
        "Fourth, the allied left. Barrios pushed the Brazilians almost back to the camp. Osório threw in the reserves and Sampaio's 3rd Division held the flank. The cavalry of General João Manuel Mena Barreto was sent to the left, surrounded the attackers and destroyed them.",
        "Fifth, the right. Resquín defeated the Argentine cavalry of Generals Cáceres and Hornos, but did not manage to decide the battle. It was a succession of charges and countercharges, and the allies' firepower prevailed.",
        "Sixth, the retreat. Around 4 or 4:30 p.m., the Paraguayans withdrew. The allies did not pursue them: they had few horses, little transport and few supplies."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Allied losses were between 3,900 and 4,300, with about 1,000 dead, most of them Brazilians. Paraguay's are much more uncertain: about 13,000 killed and wounded, according to the most cited sources, with some 6,000 dead. Estimates of the dead range from 'more than 3,000', in an order of Osório's, to about 7,000.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the last great Paraguayan attack in the open field. From then on López defended, entrenched, the line of fortresses at Curuzú, Curupaiti and Humaitá. The allies stayed camped until September, and [[cholera|cólera]] and other diseases killed about 10,000 of them.",
        "Today Brazil calls Tuiuti the 'Battle of the Patrons': Osório is the patron of the cavalry, Sampaio of the infantry, and Mallet of the artillery, all on the same battlefield.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The 'largest battle' in South America: this is a much-repeated claim, with more than 50,000 men involved. Since the sources differ on the numbers, it is a title that is repeated more than it is measured." },
        { note: "The order not to pursue: officers such as Antônio de Sena Madureira and Bernardino Bormann strongly criticized the fact that the allies did not pursue the Paraguayans. The commanders answered that they lacked horses, transport and supplies." },
        { note: "Army Day: for about four decades, 24 May was the great commemorative date of the Brazilian Army." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  curupaiti: {
    war: "Paraguayan War (1864–1870)",
    hook: "Twenty thousand men advanced to the sound of bands against a ditch that no one had seen.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Tuiuti, the allies sat for months in their camp, eaten away by disease and mud. The road to Asunción passed through Humaitá, the great Paraguayan fortress, and Humaitá could be reached only by passing through a series of positions on the riverbank. The last of these positions before the fortress was Curupaiti.",
        { h: "The road to Curupaiti" },
        { timeline: [
          ["24 May 1866", "Tuiuti: López loses the last great attack in the open field and goes over to the defense."],
          ["Early Sept. 1866", "The allies take the fort of Curuzú, on the riverbank."],
          ["8 Sept. 1866", "Mitre and López meet at Yataytí-Corá. There is no agreement, and some think López only wanted to gain time to fortify Curupaiti."],
          ["22 Sept. 1866", "The battle of Curupaiti."]
        ]},
        "Curupaiti lay about 5.5 km south of Humaitá. Its right side rested on the Paraguay River, and on the other side there was dense forest and flooded ground. Landing there was not possible, and by land the terrain was a swamp."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "José Eduvigis Díaz, Paraguayan commander" },
        "He commanded the position with about 5,000 men. He had fortified Curupaiti with two lines of [[trenches|trincheira]] and well-hidden guns. His fort was about 5.5 km from Humaitá and was the last defense before it.",
        { h: "Bartolomé Mitre, president of Argentina and commander of the allies" },
        "He commanded the allied army at Curupaiti. After the naval bombardment, he believed the Paraguayan positions had been destroyed and ordered the advance. This was the central error of the day: the order came from what he assumed, not from what he had seen.",
        { h: "Admiral Tamandaré" },
        "He commanded the Brazilian fleet, which was to bombard the fort before the attack. The fleet fired for hours, but the fort was high, on a bluff, and the shots went over it."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the allied side, about 20,000 men (11,000 Brazilians and 9,000 Argentines, according to one source), supported by eight ironclads and three gunboats. On the Paraguayan side, some 5,000 men and between 49 and 90 guns, depending on the source: one speaks of 49, another of 32 to 35 guns against the river and 58 against the land.",
        { h: "The fortification" },
        "Two lines of trenches protected the artillery. The outer one was narrow, and the inner one deeper and wider. On the outside there was a barrier of trees with sharpened points, the [[abatis|abatis]]. The fort stood on high ground above the river, and that spoiled the ships' aim.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the fleet. At about seven in the morning the ships began to bombard and continued for about five hours. About 5,000 shots were fired and they destroyed one gun. The ships had to stay out of range of the guns at Humaitá, and that made their aim worse.",
        "Second, the mistake. The end of the bombardment was read by the allied commanders as a sign that the defenses had been destroyed. It was false: the Paraguayan guns, largely hidden, were intact.",
        "Third, the advance. Around noon, the columns, Argentine and Brazilian, advanced in parade order, to the sound of bands and clarinets, with the officers in dress uniform. The ground was flooded, muddy and covered in brush.",
        "Fourth, the first trench. Under grapeshot fire, the allied troops crossed the first trench, which was narrow. The Brazilians were surprised by the second, deeper and wider, and were trapped between the two, in the mud, under fire.",
        "Fifth, the second error. Mitre thought the Brazilians had already passed the main line and sent a second wave of Argentines. It was a massacre. About 40 to 60 men managed to touch the parapet, and they were killed. There is disagreement over whether some entered the fort and took guns.",
        "Sixth, the retreat. The attack was abandoned at about 2 p.m., and by nightfall the columns were returning to Curuzú in disorder. Mitre formalized the order to retreat only after it was already happening."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The difference in losses is enormous. The allies lost, according to the most cited source, 4,227 men (2,225 Argentines and 2,002 Brazilians), about a fifth of the force. Another list gives 3,368 (998 dead and 2,370 wounded). There are much higher estimates. The Paraguayans lost between 92 and about 250, depending on the source.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the greatest allied defeat of the war. Operations were stalled for about ten months, until July 1867. On 10 October 1866, the Marquis of Caxias was appointed commander of the Brazilian forces. The defeat opened quarrels within the alliance: the Brazilian general Porto Alegre blamed Mitre, Argentina thought of negotiating a truce, and Dom Pedro II refused any peace with López outside the treaty.",
        "For Paraguay, the victory gave morale and became a symbol of national unity. López, however, did not counterattack. Curupaiti fell only in March 1868.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The Paraguayan numbers: the sources give 49 or 90 guns and losses of 92 or about 250 men. On the allied side, the counts range from about 3,400 to more than 4,000, and there are much larger estimates." },
        { note: "The deserters: after the battle, López decreed that the families of deserters would be executed, which, according to one source, reduced desertions." },
        { note: "Yataytí-Corá: the conference of 8 September, at which López and Mitre met, may have been a maneuver by López to gain time and finish the fortifications. Historians debate it." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  humaita: {
    war: "Paraguayan War (1864–1870)",
    hook: "At dawn, six iron ships passed one of the greatest fortresses in South America and the war changed course.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Humaitá was the key to Paraguay. The fortress stood on a tight bend of the Paraguay River, shaped like a horseshoe, with about 2,500 yards (about 2.3 km) of river between the banks. The guns, on top of a bluff, covered the whole bend. It became known as the 'Sebastopol of America', in a comparison with the Russian fortress of the Crimean War.",
        "On the land side, the fortress had some 13 km of trenches and flooded ground. It was garrisoned by an enormous number of guns: sources speak of anything from more than a hundred to about 400. An iron chain, supported by pontoons, could close the river.",
        { h: "The road to the passage" },
        { timeline: [
          ["22 Sept. 1866", "The defeat at Curupaiti halts the allies."],
          ["10 Oct. 1866", "The Marquis of Caxias is appointed commander of the Brazilian forces."],
          ["1867", "The ironclads pass Curupaiti and sit idle before Humaitá. Admiral Inácio hesitates to advance."],
          ["Feb. 1868", "Three monitors arrive: Alagoas, Pará and Rio Grande."],
          ["19 Feb. 1868", "The passage of Humaitá."],
          ["July 1868", "The fortress is abandoned by the Paraguayans and occupied by the allies."]
        ]},
        "The Brazilian government pressed Admiral Inácio, who preferred to wait. The one who took charge of the operation was his son-in-law, Delfim Carlos de Carvalho. Caxias, in April 1867, had said that a frontal attack on Humaitá would be madness and would lead to certain defeat."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Delfim Carlos de Carvalho" },
        "A naval officer, son-in-law of Admiral Inácio. He was given the mission of forcing the passage and carried it out. For that he became Baron of Passagem.",
        { h: "Caxias" },
        "Commander of the Brazilian forces since October 1866, he thought that a frontal attack on Humaitá would be madness. His plan was to surround the fortress by land while the fleet cut the river.",
        { h: "Solano López" },
        "He had fortified Humaitá for years and considered it impregnable. After the passage, López escaped with much of the army and the artillery through the Chaco and set up new positions on the other bank, leaving only a small force in Humaitá."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Six iron ships: the [[ironclads|encouraçado]] Barroso, Bahia and Tamandaré, the stronger ones, and the [[monitors|monitor]] Rio Grande, Alagoas and Pará. The three ironclads towed the monitors, and each pair was lashed side by side.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The river, which was exceptionally full, with 12 to 15 feet of water over the chains. This, according to some of the sources, was decisive. The [[Chaco|chaco]], on the bank opposite Humaitá, was a marshy plain, and the Paraguayans had placed a battery there, at Timbó, with 8-inch and 32-pounder guns.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the ships get ready and the land troops make a diversion. General Argolo attacks the Cierva redoubt. The attack costs the Brazilian infantry about 1,200 men, according to one source, and serves to draw attention away.",
        "Second, at about 4 in the morning, the first pair, Barroso and Rio Grande, enters the bend, with a rocket giving the signal. The Paraguayans light bonfires and fire rockets on the Chaco bank, to confuse the pilots.",
        "Third, the second pair, Bahia and Alagoas, comes right behind. A shot cuts the cable that joined them, and the Alagoas tries alone, with a damaged engine. She manages to advance and gets through at about 5:30.",
        "Fourth, the third pair, Tamandaré and Pará, passes under fire. The Tamandaré is hit three times in the bow and begins to take on water.",
        "Fifth, after Humaitá, the ships face the Timbó battery, more dangerous than the fortress itself, because it stood close to the water's surface. A table records about 110 impacts from Timbó on the Tamandaré alone. Paraguayan canoes try to board the Alagoas.",
        "Sixth, all six get through. The Tamandaré is deliberately run aground at Tayí, so as not to sink. Three ships stop for repairs and three go on toward Asunción, which López had already ordered emptied."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The six ships got through. Casualties among the crews were small (one source speaks of ten wounded), although a table adds up more than 300 impacts on the ships. The damage was mainly to the upper parts of the monitors Alagoas and Pará.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "With the ships above Humaitá, the fortress was no longer the only gateway of the river, and López ordered the army to cross the Chaco to new positions. Humaitá was abandoned and occupied in July 1868. The road to Asunción was open, but López escaped with most of the army, and the war lasted two more years, until his death at Cerro Corá, on 1 March 1870.",
        "The passage restored the prestige of the Brazilian fleet, which had been under pressure from the government to advance, and some authors see it as the turning point of the war. The commander was rewarded with the title of baron.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The chain: the sources do not agree about it. A British observer speaks of three great chains over ten pontoons. Another, of three barges. The Englishman Thompson, who served López, says that the chains sank gradually, over months, and ended up buried in the mud, without closing the river." },
        { note: "Monitors or ironclads: some sources call all six monitors, but the Barroso and the Tamandaré were coastal-defense ironclads, and only the other three were monitors." },
        { note: "The false attack: the diversionary attack on Cierva cost the Brazilian infantry about 1,200 men, according to one source. The same table gives about 150 casualties on the Paraguayan side." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  avai: {
    war: "Paraguayan War (1864–1870), the Piquissiri campaign",
    hook: "In a storm that soaked the gunpowder, the Brazilian infantry and cavalry finished the fight with steel.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In July 1868, Humaitá fell, but López escaped with the army and entrenched himself on a new line, on the Piquissiri stream.",
        "Caxias, by then a marquis, decided to go around behind it. In November 1868, engineers opened a road through the [[Chaco|chaco]], of about 11 km (other sources say more), in about 23 days. The army crossed the river and re-embarked in order to land on the Paraguayan bank, behind the Piquissiri line.",
        { h: "The Dezembrada" },
        { timeline: [
          ["July 1868", "Humaitá is occupied, but López escapes and entrenches at Piquissiri."],
          ["Nov. 1868", "Caxias orders the Chaco road opened to get around the Paraguayan line."],
          ["6 Dec. 1868", "Itororó: the Brazilians take the bridge. Some sources give the 5th."],
          ["11 Dec. 1868", "Avaí."],
          ["21–27 Dec. 1868", "Lomas Valentinas, López's last redoubt."],
          ["30 Dec. 1868", "The Angostura garrison surrenders."],
          ["1 Jan. 1869", "The allies enter Asunción."]
        ]},
        "The set of December combats became known as the Dezembrada. Avaí was the middle one, and the one that finished off General Caballero's divisions."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Caxias" },
        "Luís Alves de Lima e Silva, then Marquis of Caxias, had commanded the Brazilian forces since October 1866. The Piquissiri maneuver was his trademark: instead of attacking the position, he went around it. He became a duke only in March 1869.",
        { h: "Osório" },
        "General Manuel Luís Osório commanded the 3rd Corps, which formed the vanguard. At Avaí he opened the attack in the center and was wounded in the face by a rifle bullet. Tradition speaks of the chin or jaw. He remained in command of the cavalry to the end.",
        { h: "Andrade Neves and Mena Barreto" },
        "Joaquim de Andrade Neves and João Manuel Mena Barreto were cavalry officers. They attacked both flanks of the Paraguayan position and cut off the retreat.",
        { h: "Bernardino Caballero" },
        "He commanded the Paraguayan force defending the crossing of the Avaí stream, after fighting at Itororó a week earlier and falling back. His mission was to hold the Brazilians while López gathered forces at Lomas Valentinas."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Brazilians had about 18,900 men and 26 guns, in three army corps: the 3rd, Osório's, in the vanguard; the 2nd, José Luís Mena Barreto's, in the center; and the 1st, Jacinto Machado Bittencourt's, in the rear, with four cavalry divisions in support. The Paraguayans, 5,000 to 7,000 men and 18 guns, depending on the source.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Caballero waited on the other side of the Avaí stream, in a position chosen by the same criterion as Itororó: the creek between him and the Brazilians.",
        { h: "The battle in five movements" },
        "First, on the night of 10 December, the Brazilian army camped in Paraguayan territory, at Porto Ipané. At daybreak, Caxias ordered the march toward Villeta. He found Caballero on the other side of the Avaí stream.",
        "Second, Caxias ordered an immediate attack. Osório opened the fight in the center, and the Paraguayans resisted with great energy.",
        "Third, a violent storm broke, at the worst moment of the fight. The ground flooded, the stream rose and the attackers' powder got wet. The Brazilians had to fight with cold steel. Several officers died at the head of charges.",
        "Fourth, Caxias personally directed a strong attack by the 2nd Corps on the Paraguayan right. Andrade Neves and João Manuel Mena Barreto, with the cavalry, struck both flanks and broke the position.",
        "Fifth, the fight lasted about five hours. When the cavalry closed off the retreat, Caballero's force was destroyed. The Brazilians rested at Villeta, on the bank of the Paraguay River."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Brazilian losses were about 1,663 men (297 dead and 1,366 wounded), according to one source. Paraguayan losses were about 3,600 (3,000 dead and 600 wounded). Other counts say that only some 200 of Caballero's men survived, and one says that fewer than 50 returned to Lomas Valentinas. The sources do not agree.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Avaí destroyed Caballero's force. Ten days later, the Dezembrada continued with Lomas Valentinas and the surrender of Angostura. Asunción was occupied on 1 January 1869. The war, however, did not end: López fled into the interior and resisted until March 1870.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date: most sources give 11 December 1868, but one gives 15 December, and one account confuses the dates with those of Itororó, on 6 December." },
        { note: "The numbers: the size of the Paraguayan force varies from 5,000 to 7,000 men, and the number of survivors from fewer than 50 to 200." },
        { note: "The painting: the painter Pedro Américo made 'The Battle of Avaí' over about five years (1872–1877), on a canvas of 6 by 11 meters. It is now in the National Museum of Fine Arts, in Rio de Janeiro." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  magdala: {
    war: "British expedition to Abyssinia (1867–1868)",
    hook: "Britain sent 13,000 soldiers, 44 elephants and a railway after a handful of hostages.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the mid-19th century, Ethiopia (then called Abyssinia by Europeans) was a Christian kingdom surrounded by Muslim neighbors and divided among regional lords. In 1855, a leader of armed bands, Kassa Hailu, defeated his rivals and was crowned emperor with the name Tewodros II (Theodore, in English). He wanted to unite the country, create a professional army and modernize its weapons.",
        "For that he needed European technicians. Around 1862, he wrote to Queen Victoria asking for craftsmen who would teach how to make weapons. The letter went unanswered in the Foreign Office archives. Irritated, Theodore imprisoned the British consul Charles Cameron, the missionary Henry Stern and other Europeans, and later also the mission of Hormuzd Rassam, sent to negotiate for them. The Europeans were forced to work in a cannon foundry.",
        { h: "The road to Magdala" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1855", "Kassa Hailu is crowned emperor as Tewodros II."],
          ["c. 1862", "He writes to Queen Victoria asking for technicians. The letter goes unanswered."],
          ["21 Aug. 1867", "The queen approves a military expedition to free the hostages."],
          ["Dec. 1867", "The British land at Annesley Bay, on the Red Sea."],
          ["Feb. 1868", "The march into the interior begins, from Senafe."],
          ["10 Apr. 1868", "Arogye: the first open combat."],
          ["13 Apr. 1868", "The assault on Magdala."]
        ]},
        "The expedition was a gigantic logistical operation. About 13,000 British and Indian soldiers, 26,000 porters and other followers of the army, and more than 40,000 animals, among them 44 elephants brought from India to carry the heavy guns. They were transported by about 280 ships. The engineers built a pier, a short railway (sources say 10 to 32 km) and a road to Senafe. The commander, General Robert Napier, was an engineer, an unusual choice for a campaign. The British paid for supplies in Maria Theresa thalers, instead of looting."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Tewodros II, emperor of Ethiopia" },
        "Born around 1818, he began as a bandit chief and rose by force. He had big plans: paid governors and judges, a standing army, regular taxes. This made him enemies, above all in the Church, when he tried to tax church lands. He moved the capital from Gondar to Debre Tabor and then to the fortress of Magdala. By 1868, his army had shrunk: from about 10,000 men in mid-1867 to some 4,000 at the final hour, through desertions.",
        { h: "Robert Napier" },
        "A military engineer, he commanded the expedition. He won the support of Kassai, the future emperor Yohannes IV, and other local lords blocked escape routes. When it was over, he was made Baron Napier of Magdala."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Napier had about 13,000 men, but only some 4,000 went to the final assault. He carried breech-loading Armstrong guns, Snider rifles and rockets. The 44 elephants carried the heavy guns over mountain terrain. Theodore had between 4,000 and 10,000 men, depending on the source, with some guns and a large bronze [[mortar|morteiro]].",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Magdala stood on a rock about 90 m high above the surrounding plain, some 630 km from the Red Sea. Before reaching it, the British had to clear the neighboring heights of the Islamgee plateau, among them Selassie and Fala.",
        { h: "The battle in eight movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, on 10 April, a Good Friday, the British columns went down to the Bashilo River and climbed the Arogye plateau, facing Mount Fala. Theodore watched from the heights.",
        "Second, at about 4 in the afternoon, Theodore attacked across the plateau with about 6,000 men, under the fitawrari Gebreye, supported by the guns. The gunners misjudged the range right at the start.",
        "Third, the Snider rifles, the Armstrong guns and the British rockets made the difference. In about an hour and a half, the Ethiopians fell back. Losses were some 500 to 700 dead and many wounded. The British had about 20 wounded.",
        "Fourth, after the defeat, Theodore ordered the European [[hostages|refém]] released, but refused unconditional surrender. Napier promised him dignified treatment, and he would not accept being a prisoner.",
        "Fifth, the British cavalry surrounded the Magdala mountain to close off escape, and the infantry and guns climbed Mount Selassie.",
        "Sixth, on 13 April, after the deadline of a truce ran out, the British bombarded the fortress and advanced. An assault party of the 33rd Regiment, backed by engineers and sappers, reached the gate under fire. The engineers discovered they had forgotten the powder to blow it up. A soldier of the 33rd found another way: he climbed the wall, and the men who followed him overpowered the defenders at the gate.",
        "Seventh, the gate was opened and the main force went in. The garrison surrendered.",
        "Eighth, inside the second gate the British found Theodore dead. He had killed himself with a pistol shot, a pistol that Queen Victoria had given him."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The hostages were freed. In combat, British losses were small: about 20 wounded at Arogye and very few in the taking of Magdala. The Ethiopians lost some 700 dead and 1,200 wounded at Arogye. The expedition cost close to 9 million pounds, according to one historian, a fortune for the time.",
        "Magdala was looted and burned. The British left Ethiopia by mid-June.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Two soldiers of the 33rd, Bergin and Magner, received the Victoria Cross for the assault. It was one of the most expensive and ambitious operations of the British army in the 19th century, and it showed that the European powers could carry an entire force to a distant plateau. Napier gave about 500,000 pounds in weapons and supplies to Kassai, who won the contest for the throne and became the emperor Yohannes IV. Theodore's son, Prince Alemayehu, was taken to England, educated at Rugby and Sandhurst and died in 1879, aged 19.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The looting: the spoils, which included manuscripts, crowns and religious objects, were auctioned, and the British Museum took more than 300 manuscripts. Ethiopia asks for the rest back to this day: some items were returned in 1924, in the 1960s, in 2019, 2021 and 2023, and the British Museum still holds 11 [[tabots|tabot]]. Ethiopia also asks for the remains of Alemayehu, which are at Windsor." },
        { note: "The Ethiopian prisoners: according to the accounts, Theodore had hundreds of prisoners from his own people thrown off the cliff (one source speaks of about 300), which hardened Napier's attitude." },
        { note: "The death of Theodore: most sources give 13 April, but one biography says 15 April. We use the day of the assault." },
        { note: "The numbers: the sources give the British army as 12,000 to 13,000 men and Theodore's as 4,000 to 10,000 (one list reaches 32,000–40,000 for Napier's). The distance marched ranges from 400 miles to about 640 km." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

